Ab chalte hain iske sabse interesting part par—Filesystem Hierarchy.
Windows mein hume aadat hoti hai C: Drive aur D: Drive dekhne ki. Linux mein koi drives nahi hoti. 
Linux mein ek hi directory hai—Root Directory (/). Sab kuch / ke niche hi rehta hai! 
/ (Root)
├── bin/    → Essential commands (ls, cp, mv)
├── etc/    → Configuration files ka ghar!
├── home/   → Normal users ki personal directories
├── var/    → Variable data (Logs, temp data)
├── dev/    → Hardware device files
└── proc/   → Virtual directory (Kernel ka Live memory)

/etc: System aur services ke saare config files yahan milenge (e.g., /etc/nginx/)
/var/log: Jab bhi koi server crash ho, yahan jaakar log check karo.

Yahan poora system ek badi multi-story building ki tarah hai jiska ek hi main entrance hai. Is main darwaze ko hum kehte hain Root, jise / (forward slash) se likha jata hai. 
Is darwaze ke andar alag-alag fixed rooms (directories) hain, jinka apna ek specific kaam hai:

Jaise /bin —ye building ka tool-room hai, jahan saari basic commands rakhi hain.
Phir aata hai /etc—ye poore system ka control room hai. Aapke system ki, web servers ki saari configuration files yahin milti hain.
Ek room hai /var/log—ye system ka CCTV aur diary hai. System mein jab bhi koi error aati hai ya server crash hota hai, toh developer seedha is room mein aakar logs check karta hai.
Aur ek hota hai /home—ye har user ka apna personal locker ya desk hai jahan wo apna data rakhta hai.


Absolute vs Relative Paths
Path samajhna bohot zaroori hai. Do tariqe hain:
Absolute Path: Hamesha / (Root) se start hota hai. Full address!
Example: /home/alice/documents/report.txt
Relative Path: Aap abhi jahan khade ho, wahan se rasta batata hai.
Example: documents/report.txt
Shortcuts jo life easy banate hain:
~ (Tilde) = Aapka Home directory.
. (Single Dot) = Current directory.
.. (Double Dot) = Ek step peechhe (Parent directory).
- (Dash) = Pichli directory mein wapas jane ke liye (cd -).

